🎯 The Big Picture First

Before designing ANY database, you need to figure out: what THINGS exist in your system, what INFO do you store about each thing, and HOW do these things relate to each other? That's literally all an ER (Entity-Relationship) diagram is — a visual blueprint of your database BEFORE you write a single line of SQL.

Analogy: Think of it like planning a wedding guest list before booking tables 💍. You figure out WHO's coming (entities), WHAT you need to know about each guest — dietary restrictions, plus-one, table preference (attributes) — and HOW guests relate to each other — "this person is the bride's cousin," "these two are a couple" (relationships). You plan ALL this on paper before actually seating anyone. That's exactly what ER diagrams do for databases.

1️⃣ Entity — "A THING worth tracking" 📦

The rule: An entity is any real-world object or concept that you want to store data about — a person, place, thing, or event. In a database, an entity eventually becomes a TABLE.

Real analogy: In a college database — Student, Professor, Course are all entities. Each one is a distinct "thing" you'd track separately.

In diagrams: Represented as a rectangle 🟦

┌─────────┐
| Student |
└─────────┘

Two types of entities:

Entity Type — the general category, like "Student" (the blueprint)
Entity Instance — one specific example, like "Sangram Khandagale" (an actual row that will exist in the table)

🎤 One-liner: "An entity represents a distinct real-world object about which we store data, and it typically maps to a table in the resulting database."

2️⃣ Attribute — "Info ABOUT the thing" 📝

The rule: Attributes are properties/characteristics that describe an entity. These become the COLUMNS of the table.

Real analogy: For our Student entity — attributes would be Name, Roll Number, Email, CGPA. These are the specific PIECES of info you'd want to know about each student.

In diagrams: Represented as ovals connected to the entity 🥚

        (Name)
          |
┌─────────┐──(Roll Number)
| Student |
└─────────┘──(Email)
          |
        (CGPA)

Types of attributes (this is where interviewers dig deeper):

Simple attribute — can't be broken down further. E.g., Age (just a number, that's it)
Composite attribute — CAN be broken into sub-parts. E.g., Address = Street + City + Pincode. In the diagram, this shows as an oval with smaller ovals branching off it
Single-valued attribute — holds only ONE value. E.g., Date of Birth (you only have one)
Multi-valued attribute — CAN hold multiple values. E.g., Phone Numbers (someone might have 2-3 numbers). Shown as a double oval in diagrams ⭕⭕
Derived attribute — calculated FROM another attribute, not stored directly. E.g., Age can be derived from Date of Birth — shown as a dashed oval

🎤 One-liner: "Attributes are properties that describe an entity and become columns in the resulting table — they can be simple, composite, single-valued, multi-valued, or derived."

3️⃣ Relationship — "How TWO entities connect" 🔗

The rule: A relationship shows how two (or more) entities are associated with each other. In diagrams, represented as a diamond 💎 connecting the related entities.

Real analogy: Student ENROLLS IN Course — that "ENROLLS IN" is the relationship connecting two separate entities.

┌─────────┐          ┌───────────┐          ┌────────┐
| Student |──────────| ENROLLS_IN|──────────| Course |
└─────────┘          └───────────┘          └────────┘
🎯 Cardinality — the part interviewers REALLY test

This describes HOW MANY of one entity can relate to HOW MANY of another. Three main types:

One-to-One (1:1) 1️⃣↔️1️⃣
Analogy: One person, one Aadhaar card. A person has exactly ONE Aadhaar, and one Aadhaar belongs to exactly ONE person.

One-to-Many (1:N) 1️⃣↔️🔢
Analogy: One Professor teaches MANY Courses, but each Course (in a simple model) has just ONE professor. This is genuinely the MOST common relationship type in real systems.

Many-to-Many (M:N) 🔢↔️🔢
Analogy: Student ENROLLS IN Course — one student can enroll in MANY courses, AND one course has MANY students. This is the trickiest one because in an actual SQL database, you CAN'T directly implement M:N — you need a junction/bridge table in between (e.g., an Enrollment table with student_id and course_id) to break it into two 1:N relationships. 🔥 This is a classic interview follow-up, so remember it.

4️⃣ Weak Entity — "Can't stand on its own" 🩹

The rule: A weak entity is one that CANNOT be uniquely identified by its own attributes alone — it depends on a STRONG (parent) entity to exist. It doesn't have its own primary key; instead, it uses a partial key combined with the parent's key.

Real analogy: Think of Room inside a House 🏠. A room number like "Room 2" isn't unique across the WHOLE WORLD — tons of houses have a "Room 2." But combine it with WHICH house — "Room 2 of House #45" — NOW it's unique. The Room entity literally cannot exist meaningfully without being tied to a specific House. If the house gets deleted, the room record becomes meaningless too (notice — this is basically Composition from OOP, applied to databases! 🤯).

Another classic example: Dependent (like a family member on an insurance policy) depends on Employee. A dependent named "Raj" isn't unique on his own — but "Raj, dependent of Employee #123" is unique.

In diagrams:

Weak entity = double-bordered rectangle ⬜⬜
The relationship connecting it to its parent = double-bordered diamond 💎💎
Its partial key (discriminator) = dashed underline on the attribute (instead of solid underline for a normal primary key)
┌──────────┐         ┌════════════┐        ╔═══════╗
|  House   |─────────‖ CONTAINS   ‖────────‖ Room  ║
└──────────┘         ╚════════════╝        ╚═══════╝

(Double borders = weak entity + identifying relationship)

🎤 One-liner: "A weak entity is one that cannot be uniquely identified by its own attributes and depends on a strong entity's primary key, combined with its own partial key, to form a unique identifier — represented with double-bordered rectangles and diamonds in ER diagrams."